%N A400295 Successor function of Conway's POLYGAME FRACTRAN program, with -1 for values where it isn't defined.
%C A400295 In contrast to Conway's PRIMEGAME (A203907, A202138/A203363) and PIGAME (A395539, A350555/A350556), where the denominator of the last fraction is 1 so that the successor function is defined for any input value, this isn't the case for POLYGAME, partially justified by its definition.
%C A400295 The POLYGAME program consists of iterations of this successor function. It selects catalog numbers for computable functions as follows: if for an initial value c*2^2^k it stops at 2^2^m, then c is (by definition) the "catalog number" of the function f_c such that f_c(k) = m; else c isn't the catalog number of any computable function.
%C A400295 A FRACTRAN program consists of a list of fractions [f[1], ..., f[N]]. The associated successor function is defined as f(x) = x * f[i(x)], where i(x) = min {k >= 1 : x*f[k] is an integer}. Alternatively, i(x) = min {k >= 1 : d[k] | x}, where d[k] = denominator(f[k]). This i(x) exists only if one of the d[k] indeed divides x, else f(x) isn't defined for x.
%H A400295 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400295/b400295.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A400295 John H. Conway, "FRACTRAN: A Simple Universal Programming Language for Arithmetic", in T. M. Cover and B. Gopinath, eds, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-4808-8_2">Open Problems in Communication and Computation</a>, Springer, New York, NY, 1987, pp. 4-26.
%H A400295 John H. Conway, "FRACTRAN: A Simple Universal Programming Language for Arithmetic", in J. C. Lagarias, ed., <a href="http://www.ams.org/bookstore-getitem/item=mbk-78">The Ultimate Challenge: The 3x+1 Problem</a>, American Mathematical Society, 2010, pp. 249-264.
%H A400295 M. F. Hasler, <a href="http://github.com/m-f-h/OEIS/blob/main/A202138%20etc.%3A%20FRACTRAN%20programs.md">Code for FRACTRAN programs</a>, on GitHub.com, Sept. 2026
%H A400295 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FRACTRAN">FRACTRAN</a>.
%e A400295 For input n = 0, any FRACTRAN successor function yields a(0) = 0 since multiplying with the first fraction of the list gives the integer 0. For input n = 1, 2, 4 and 5 there is no denominator listed in A350664 that divides n, so the POLYGAME successor function is undefined, represented by a(n) = -1.
%e A400295 For input n = 3, we have A350664[24] = 3 which divides, hence a(3) = 3/A350664[24]*A350663[24] = 1.
%e A400295 Similarly, for input n = 6, A350664[24] = 3 divides again, hence a(6) = 6/3*1 = 2.
%e A400295 For input n = 7, we have A350664[21] = 7 which divides, hence a(7) = 7/A350664[21]*A350663[21] = 159, and so on.
%e A400295 Conway (1987) gives some examples of catalog numbers c = 0 corresponds to the function f_0 which is defined for no input at all. The catalog number c = 1 corresponds to the identity function f_1: k -> k for all nonnegative integers k: indeed, the starting value 1*2^2^k is already of the form 2^2^m with m = k.
%e A400295 The catalog number c = 2 corresponds to the function f_2: 0 -> 1: the starting value 2*2^2^0 = 2^2 = 2^2^m with m = 1, and the function f_2 does not return a result for any other input k > 0.
%e A400295 The catalog number c = 77 corresponds to the zero function f_77: k -> 0 for all k >= 0. Indeed, consider a starting value 77*T, with T = 2^2^k: the first fraction that gives an integer is f[21] = 159/7, giving 159*11*T. Next, the fraction f[9] = 43/53 gives the integer 3*11*43*T. Then, the fraction f[6] = 7*53/(3*43) gives the integer 7*11*53*T. Then, f[9] = 43/53 gives the integer 7*11*43*T. Now, if T >= 2, then f[8] = 53/86 is used, which adds a factor of 53, which is then exchanged for a factor 43 through f[9]. This repeats until there is no factor 2 left. Then we get 7*11*41, then 7*11*47, 2*7*41, 2*7*47, 2*3*5*41, 2*3*5*47, 2*3*5*23, 2*3 and finally 2 = 2^2^0, so the result is zero.
%t A400295 polygame = {583/559, 629/551, 437/527, 82/517, 615/329, 371/129, 1/115, 53/86, 43/53, 23/47, 341/46, 41/43, 47/41, 29/37, 37/31, 37/31, 299/29, 47/23, 161/15, 527/19, 159/7, 1/17, 1/13, 1/3};
%t A400295 Array[SelectFirst[#*polygame, IntegerQ, -1] &, 100, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 23 2026 *)
%o A400295 (PARI) /* naïve code, see LINKS for more efficient code */
%o A400295 A400295(n) = -!for(i=1, #A350664, n%A350664[i]|| return(n\A350664[i]*A350663[i]))
